// DM mission files: the Lea Boys (LB1–LB8). Checks: [stat or 'a|b', band, when, pass, fail].
const DEN = {name: 'Big Den', role: 'Bow Breakers Yard, Roman Road', img: 'systems/the-smoke/assets/tokens/people/ped-17.webp'};
export default {
  lb1: {
    brief: `It should be a simple collection. It isn't. Daniel Firth owes the Lea Boys, and the debt is real: Moth has seen the paper. But his wife has a receipt saying it's paid, and a second collector is out demanding the same money all over again. Someone has sold the same debt twice. Moth wants it straightened out before Daz decides to make an example of somebody. The twist: the broker bought the debt from Vic Randle's loan office. Vic has been selling Lea Bridge debts on the quiet.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: Moth's brief`,
    open: `The counting office at the Lea Bridge Social Rooms is a cupboard with a desk in it. Moth sits behind cash bags and three notebooks, hair scraped back, a mug of builder's tea going cold at her elbow. She slides a sheet of paper across. "Easy one," she says. "Bloke owes. Proper debt, I've seen the paper. Except his missus has a receipt that says it's paid." She taps the paper. "And now someone else is collecting the same money again. So either he's mugging us, or someone's mugging him." She picks up her tea. "Sort out who's mugging who. Before Daz does."`,
    cast: ['tessa-price', 'daniel-firth', 'frankie-wu'],
    people: {'daz-riley': {}},
    roles: {'tessa-price': `Moth. Wants the debt straightened out quietly, and wants to know who is selling Lea Boys paper behind her back.`, 'daniel-firth': `The debtor. A committee clerk with a minute book, a receipt and a wife who is very angry with him.`, 'frankie-wu': `An accountant who keeps the books at the Shepherdess Coin Rooms. Can find the gap in any set of numbers.`, 'daz-riley': `Wants an example made. Cracks his knuckles a lot.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'WS3.2', title: `The friendly chat at The Gun`, read: `The Gun on Well Street is a proper local: dartboard, horse brasses, a landlord who has seen everything. In the snug, a man in a tweed jacket with elbow patches sits very upright with a minute book on the table in front of him, as if he's about to chair a meeting. His wife sits beside him with a receipt in a plastic sleeve. Daz Riley leans against the fruit machine, cracking his knuckles one at a time. "Friendly chat," Daz says. "Just a friendly chat."`,
        text: `Daniel Firth paid his debt to a collector in March: the receipt is genuine, on Lea Boys paper, signed. But Moth's book still shows it owed, and a second collector came last week. Daz wants to lean on him anyway. The neighbours are watching through the window.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'medium', `Stand between Daz and the Firths when he steps forward`, `Daz stops, looks at you, and goes back to the fruit machine, sulking.`, `Daz shoves past you. Mrs Firth screams. The landlord reaches for the phone.`],
          ['mus', 'medium', `If Daz won't stop, hold him back`, `You pin his arm to the bar until he calms down.`, `He throws you off. Now it's a scrap.`],
          ['frt', 'medium', `Get the Firths to tell you who they paid`, `"Young lad. Lea Boys jacket. Said his name was Kyle." They show you the receipt: real paper, real signature.`, `Daniel quotes procedure at you for ten minutes and gives you nothing.`]]},
      {at: 'HX8.1', title: `The Shepherdess Coin Rooms`, read: `The Shepherdess Coin Rooms are an old-fashioned gambling club on Shepherdess Walk: green baize, brass lamps, and a public room where old men pitch coins at a line chalked on the floor. Closest to the wall wins the pot. Through a door at the back is the account office. A broker in a pinstripe waistcoat sits at the private table counting notes.`,
        text: `The broker who sold the second copy of the debt drinks here. The regulars won't let strangers near the back without earning it. Winning a round of coin-pitching gets the broker's attention.`,
        checks: [['aim', 'medium', `Win a round of coin-pitching against the regulars`, `Your coin lands touching the wall. The old men grumble, and the broker invites you over.`, `Short. Everyone laughs, and the broker doesn't look up.`],
          ['sav', 'medium', `Spot the broker's tell when he lies about where he got the debt`, `He touches his watch every time he says "I bought it fair". He's lying.`, `He seems sincere enough to you.`]]},
      {at: 'HX8.3', title: `The account office`, read: `The account office is a small room with a big safe, a ledger on a lectern and a woman in a tidy suit with two laptops and reading glasses on her head. Frankie Wu doesn't stop typing. "Numbers don't lie," she says. "People lie about numbers. I find the gap. For a fee."`,
        text: `Frankie can find the duplicate entry. The debt was sold twice: once by the Lea Boys' own collector (paid off in March) and once by Vic Randle's loan office, who bought a copy of Moth's book. The duplicated debt receipt is a physical prop: it's in the ledger. The broker slides a cut across the table if the crew will look the other way.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'medium', `Get the duplicate receipt out of the ledger without Frankie seeing`, `It's in your pocket. Frankie looks up a second later.`, `"Put that back, please," says Frankie, without looking up.`],
          ['btl', 'easy', `Push the broker's envelope back across the table`, `He shrugs. "Your loss." But he tells you where he bought it: Vic Randle.`, `You hesitate a second too long. He thinks you're for sale.`]]},
      {at: 'HX8.6', title: `The broker's runner`, read: `As you come out of the account office, a lad in a Lea Boys jacket bolts down the rear passage clutching a carrier bag. "That's Kyle," says Mrs Firth, who has followed you all the way from Well Street. "That's the one who took our money!" Outside, a moped engine starts.`,
        text: `Kyle is the Lea Boys' own collector, who pocketed the Firths' payment and never handed it in. He's on a moped.`,
        checks: [['whl', 'medium', `Chase Kyle's moped through Hoxton`, `You cut him off on Eagle Wharf Road. He hands over the bag: three months of pocketed payments.`, `He's gone up the towpath, where you can't follow.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `Or vault the bins in the rear passage and grab him before he's on the moped`, `You've got his collar before he gets the key in.`, `You land in the bins. He's away.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: who's mugging who`,
    close: `Moth listens to the whole story without interrupting: Kyle pocketing payments, the copy of her book, Vic Randle's broker selling it on. When you've finished, she closes her notebook very gently. "So the Firths paid," she says. "Kyle kept it. And Vic's got a copy of my book." She is quiet for a moment. "Right. Kyle I can deal with." She looks at you. "Vic Randle having a copy of my book. That's a different conversation."`,
    notes: [`The decision: collect by leaning on the Firths, buy up the genuine debt, expose the double sale, or pocket the broker's fee. Moth will take a renegotiated deal if the crew can sell it.`,
      `The duplicated debt receipt is a physical prop: collect it at the Coin Rooms.`,
      `Vic Randle selling Lea Bridge debts is the hook into the Backroom Bookmakers career (BB1).`,
      `Square it and LB2 opens, plus the counting room. Smacking someone doesn't make the receipt disappear.`,
      `The coin-pitching game is a nice table moment: let every player try it.`]
  },
  lb2: {
    brief: `Pat got her van back after M3, but Stretch says some of what was in it was already his. Now there's a crate in a depot on Orient Way, two buyers who have both paid for it, and a Lea Boys lieutenant itching to move it. The crew see Big Den at Bow Breakers, get a look at the crate in the Orient Night Shed's cold cage, stop the lieutenant moving it, and settle it at a sit-down in The Princess of Wales. The twist: the crate is empty. What everyone paid for is the paperwork inside it: lease options on six "empty" buildings.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: Moth again`,
    open: `Moth rings while walking somewhere windy. "Stretch reckons Pat's van was carrying gear that was promised to him. Before it got nicked. Before you got it back." A bus goes past. "Now he wants his cut and Pat wants him to do one. They're both saying it to everyone who'll listen." She stops walking. "Sort it before it turns into a proper war, yeah? I can't be doing a war this month. I've got my nan's birthday."`,
    cast: ['tessa-price', 'marisol-vega', 'pearl-okonkwo'],
    people: {'big-den': DEN, stretch: {}},
    roles: {'tessa-price': `Moth. Wants peace between Stretch and Pat more than she wants the crate.`, 'marisol-vega': `Runs the night shift at the Orient Night Shed. Can count crates, and wants nobody's nonsense on her floor.`, 'pearl-okonkwo': `Agrees to witness the sit-down so nobody can say they were mugged off.`, 'big-den': `Saw everything at the breaker's yard. Remembers nothing, unless you're useful.`, stretch: `Wants his cut, and wants to be seen getting it.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'EX-BREAKERS', title: `Bow Breakers Yard`, read: `Bow Breakers on Roman Road is a canyon of crushed cars stacked four high, a crane grabbing at the sky, and a Portakabin with a kettle. Big Den stands in the middle of it in cut-off overalls, goggles in his hair, trying to shift an engine block onto a pallet on his own. He doesn't stop when he sees you. "If you're here to talk, grab the other end."`,
        text: `Big Den saw Pat's van come through when M3 happened. He saw a crate taken off before the frames were unloaded, and the van it went into. He'll tell you if you're useful.`,
        checks: [['mus', 'medium', `Grab the other end of the engine block`, `It goes on the pallet with a thump. Den wipes his hands. "Blue Transit. Orient Way. Night shed."`, `It slips. Den catches it, swears at you, and says nothing.`],
          ['grt', 'easy', `Stay and help for an hour in the drizzle`, `By the end, Den's pouring you tea from a flask and telling you about Stretch's lieutenant.`, `You leave after twenty minutes. Den shrugs.`]]},
      {at: 'HM7.4', title: `The Orient Night Shed, cold cage`, read: `The Orient Night Shed is a lorry hall on Orient Way, lit like an operating theatre, with forklifts beeping and a cold cage at the back behind chain-link. A woman in hi-vis with a hairnet, a clipboard and arms like a docker stands at the cage door. "Paperwork says one crate," Marisol Vega says. "I can count, love. That's two."`,
        text: `There are two crates: one Stretch's lieutenant has paid to move, and a decoy. Getting a look means getting into the cold cage, which is minus five. The real one has a label in the name of a company nobody has heard of. Then the lieutenant arrives and starts loading it onto a second van.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'medium', `Get into the cold cage for a look at the crate`, `Through the chain-link door. The real crate has a Quayline label.`, `The lock won't give. Marisol's watching.`],
          ['grt', 'medium', `Stay in the cold cage long enough to check both crates`, `Fingers numb, but you know which is which.`, `You last two minutes and come out shivering, unsure.`],
          ['aim', 'hard', `As the second van pulls away, throw Kez's old phone (with tracking on) into its open back`, `It skids under a pallet. You can follow the van anywhere.`, `It bounces off the door and smashes on the tarmac.`],
          ['whl', 'hard', `Or chase the van out onto the A12`, `You box it in at the Eastway lights. The lieutenant gives up.`, `It's lost in the traffic on the A12.`]]},
      {at: 'CL2.2', title: `The sit-down at The Princess of Wales`, read: `The Princess of Wales is a big Victorian pub by the Lea, with a beer garden on the water. In the snug, Stretch sits with his scarf on, perfectly calm. Pearl Okonkwo sits beside him as the agreed witness, her cash bag on her belt. At the next table, a courier in a hi-vis jacket is pretending to read the Evening Standard. He hasn't turned a page in twenty minutes.`,
        text: `The crate is opened on the table. It's empty, except for a slim folder: lease options on six "empty" buildings. That's what everyone paid for. The courier is waiting to take it to whoever pays. The first buyer wants a refund, and a rival offers a recovery fee to make it vanish.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'medium', `Argue a split in front of Pearl so nobody can say they were mugged off`, `Stretch takes his cut in public. Pat takes hers. Pearl nods. It holds.`, `Stretch walks out. Pat hears about it by teatime.`],
          ['btl', 'medium', `Keep your nerve when Stretch's lieutenant reaches for the folder`, `You put your hand flat on it and don't move. He backs off.`, `He has it. Now it's a chase.`],
          ['mov', 'easy', `Stop the courier slipping out with the folder`, `You're at the door before he is.`, `He's out the beer garden and onto the towpath.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: an empty crate`,
    close: `Stretch looks into the empty crate for a long time. Then he picks up the folder, flicks through six pages of lease options, and laughs, a low, real laugh. "All this," he says, "for paper." He drops the folder on the table. "Somebody's buying Hackney one empty building at a time, and we're fighting over the box it came in." He stands up. "Tell Pat she can keep her frames."`,
    notes: [`The decision: back Stretch, give Pat her share, steal it for a third buyer, or split it in front of witnesses.`,
      `The crate is a physical prop. The lease options inside link to the six-property stitch-up (CH6, MR5).`,
      `Whoever the crew backed remembers the deal. An unpaid claim doesn't die just because the crate changed hands.`,
      `The night shed is always there if a chase goes wrong (E1). Marisol remembers who caused trouble on her floor.`]
  },
  lb3: {
    brief: `A warehouse job, proper heist. A consignment of Halo is sitting in the Larch Cold Store waiting for Stretch's buyer. Moth wants it lifted from under his nose; Owen Pike offers more if it lands with his promoters. The crew get the pickup time from a collector at The Crooked Billet, get into the cold store and reach the stock, deal with the other crew who had the same idea, and decide where it goes. The twist: the invoice shows the load was changed at the depot two days ago, signed for by somebody from "East Cross Security".`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: the job`,
    open: `Moth meets you in the rear garage at the Social Rooms, under one bare bulb, and unrolls a floor plan on the bonnet of a car. "Halo. A whole load of it, sat in the Larch Cold Store waiting for Stretch's buyer." She taps the plan. "I want it gone before he collects. Owen Pike'll pay more if it ends up with his promoters instead." She rolls the plan up and hands it to you. "Your choice. Don't get nicked."`,
    cast: ['tessa-price', 'marta-severin', 'owen-pike'],
    roles: {'tessa-price': `Moth. Wants the stock gone from under Stretch's nose.`, 'marta-severin': `The warehouse night manager. Not part of the plan, and in the wrong place at the wrong time.`, 'owen-pike': `Pays more if the goods end up with his promoters. Waits in the Blue Hour Basement's cash room.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'CL3.2', title: `The Crooked Billet`, read: `The Crooked Billet on Upper Clapton Road is a big old pub with a pool table under a Tiffany lamp. Stretch's collector, a heavy lad in a tracksuit, has had a few and is beating everyone at pool, loudly. He has a phone in his back pocket that keeps buzzing. "Next," he shouts, chalking his cue. "Tenner a frame."`,
        text: `Beat him at pool and he'll talk; get him chatting and he'll mention the pickup time (Thursday, 2am) without realising. Or lift his phone.`,
        checks: [['aim', 'medium', `Beat him at pool for a tenner`, `You sink the black off two cushions. He's so impressed he tells you the pickup time.`, `He clears up. "Next!" You're down a tenner and none the wiser.`],
          ['grf', 'medium', `Or lift his buzzing phone and read the messages`, `"THURS 2AM. LARCH. BAY 3." Phone back in his pocket.`, `He feels it go. Now he's suspicious.`]]},
      {at: 'LY8.2', title: `The Larch Cold Store`, read: `The Larch Cold Store is a white box on Orient Way with no windows and one blue light over the loading bay. At 1.30am, the lorry apron is empty. Inside: a loading hall with forklifts, a night office with a kettle and a radio, a stock room at minus twenty, and at the back, a damaged-goods cage where the case is waiting.`,
        text: `Graft for the locks, and a guard who does his rounds every fifteen minutes. Marta Severin, the night manager, is in the night office. She shouldn't be there tonight; she covered a shift. The goods case is a physical prop in the damaged-goods cage.`,
        checks: [['mov', 'medium', `Slip past the night office while the guard does his round`, `Past the window, below the sill, while Marta's kettle boils.`, `The guard turns back for his torch. You're in the open.`],
          ['grf', 'hard', `Open the damaged-goods cage`, `Click. The case is inside, with an invoice taped to the lid.`, `It won't give. You need a grinder, and grinders are loud.`],
          ['grt', 'medium', `Get through the stock room at minus twenty`, `Breath freezing, fingers numb, but you're through.`, `You come out shaking. Every roll is harder for the next ten minutes.`]]},
      {at: 'LY8.6', title: `The other crew`, read: `Headlights sweep across the loading hall. A second van, no lights on, reverses up to bay three. Four people in balaclavas climb out with bolt cutters. They see you. You see them. For a moment, nobody moves. Then the handover van rolls in early, beeping.`,
        text: `The twist arrives all at once: the rival crew, the early handover van, Marta in the night office and the invoice (signed by "East Cross Security") are all in the same place. Deal with the rival crew with muscle or a very quick deal, and get out.`,
        checks: [['mus', 'hard', `Fight the rival crew off the case`, `Two down, two run. The case is yours.`, `They take the case. You'll have to take it back.`],
          ['frt', 'medium', `Or cut a very quick deal with the rival crew`, `"Half each and nobody saw anyone." They agree.`, `They laugh. "Nah."`],
          ['whl', 'hard', `Get out of the lorry apron with the case before the handover crew blocks it`, `Through the gap as the gate closes.`, `Blocked in. It's a standoff.`]]},
      {at: 'DA8.5', title: `The Blue Hour Basement`, read: `The Blue Hour Basement on Shacklewell Lane is a club down a flight of stairs, all blue light and velvet. Behind the bar, a door leads to a cash room where Owen Pike sits in a velvet blazer, rings on every finger, a vape that smells of mango. "Love what you're doing," he says. "Love it. Let's talk about my percentage."`,
        text: `Owen's cash-for-cargo swap, if the crew go that way. He pays more than Moth, but owning a debt to Owen is its own kind of trouble.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'medium', `Hold your price with Owen`, `He pays what you asked, with a smile that doesn't reach his eyes.`, `He talks you down by a third and makes you thank him.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: East Cross Security`,
    close: `Back in the rear garage, Moth reads the invoice that was taped to the case. She reads it twice. "Changed at the depot two days ago," she says. "Signed for by somebody from East Cross Security." She looks up. "We don't use East Cross Security. Stretch doesn't use East Cross Security." She folds the invoice. "So who does?"`,
    notes: [`The decision: deliver to Moth, sell to Owen, keep it, trade it to another faction, or arrange for it to disappear quietly for compensation.`,
      `The goods case is a physical prop. The invoice is the hook for the bad-batch mystery (E2) and LB4.`,
      `East Cross Security is Rusk's front. This links to MR and LD.`,
      `Get caught selling it twice and the crew have made a very specific enemy.`,
      `Marta Severin was in the wrong place. If she gets hurt or blamed, she remembers; she appears again in LB4, VC3, VC4 and GE6.`]
  },
  lb4: {
    brief: `The Halo batch put people in hospital. Stretch wants the leftovers back on the street. Moth is convinced the Lea Boys were set up to take the blame for a switch somebody else made. The crew hear a hurt courier at the Approach Recovery Rooms, find out what the original delivery should have been at The Spread Eagle, find the changed booking at the Larch Cold Store, and get a witness to Majors at Homerton A&E. The twist: the courier who got hurt is Daz's little brother. Daz doesn't know yet.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: people got hurt`,
    open: `Moth doesn't sound like herself. "People got hurt," she says. "That batch was wrong. Three in Homerton. One's still in there." A long breath. "Stretch wants the rest of the stock back out. He's not asking." Another breath. "I reckon we got paid to take the blame for something someone else did. Prove it." A pause. "Please."`,
    cast: ['tessa-price', 'tariq-evans', 'asha-stone', 'marta-severin'],
    people: {'daz-riley': {}},
    roles: {'tessa-price': `Moth. Shaken. Wants the truth, and the stock off the street.`, 'tariq-evans': `Patient advocate at the Approach Recovery Rooms. Protects the hurt courier.`, 'asha-stone': `Retired care worker who drinks at The Spread Eagle and saw the original delivery.`, 'marta-severin': `Night manager at the Larch Cold Store. Kept the changed booking.`, 'daz-riley': `Doesn't know his little brother is the courier who got hurt.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'BG8.3', title: `The Approach Recovery Rooms`, read: `In the support room, a lad of about nineteen sits wrapped in a blanket with a cup of hot chocolate he hasn't touched. He has a cannula plaster on the back of his hand and a hospital wristband. Tariq Evans sits beside him. "He'll talk to you," Tariq says. "If you talk to him like a person."`,
        text: `The courier (Daz's little brother, Jordan) picked up the batch from a van at The Spread Eagle. The bags were a different colour from usual. He tried a bit himself to check it. He wants nobody to tell Daz.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'medium', `Talk to Jordan like a person`, `He tells you: different bags, different van, a man in a black fleece with no logo.`, `He shuts down. Tariq asks you to leave.`],
          ['sav', 'easy', `Notice the name on his hospital wristband`, `JORDAN RILEY. Daz's brother.`, `You don't see it. That'll come back later.`]]},
      {at: 'WW4.2', title: `The Spread Eagle`, read: `The Spread Eagle on Homerton High Street has a snug with a dartboard and an older woman with a crossword folded in her pocket and a carrier bag of shopping at her feet. Asha Stone looks up. "You're asking about the van," she says. "Tuesday. Two of them. One came, one went, and they swapped bags in the yard. I sat with people at the end for thirty years, love. I know when something's wrong."`,
        text: `Asha saw the switch in the service yard: the Lea Boys' van, and a second van with "East Cross Security" on the side. She'll tell you, but she wants the stock off the street.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'easy', `Promise Asha you'll get it off the street, and mean it`, `She tells you the second van's plate.`, `She tells you what she saw, but not the plate.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `Climb onto the yard wall to see the CCTV camera's angle`, `It covers the yard. The footage is on the landlord's computer.`, `The wall crumbles. You land in the bins.`]]},
      {at: 'LY8.5', title: `The Larch Cold Store, night office`, read: `Marta Severin is in the night office again, insulated jacket zipped to the chin, keys on a carabiner. She doesn't look surprised to see you. "You," she says. "From the other night." She slides a printout across the desk. "I kept this. I'm not stupid. I'm also not getting blamed."`,
        text: `The changed delivery booking is a physical prop. Marta kept a copy. It shows the booking was changed by an East Cross Security login. A collector is shifting the rest of the load right now, from the lorry apron.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'medium', `Pull the original booking from the system before it's deleted`, `Printed, with the login and time on it.`, `Deleted while you watch. Marta's copy is all there is.`],
          ['aim', 'medium', `Hit the collector's van with a thrown fire extinguisher to burst its tyre as it leaves`, `Bang. Flat. They're stuck on the apron.`, `It misses. The van's gone with the load.`]]},
      {at: 'AE3', title: `Majors, Homerton A&E`, read: `Your phone goes. Asha. "The other lad, Jordan's mate. He's here. He's collapsed. Nobody's called anyone because they're frightened." Across London, the collector is shifting the last of the stock. You can't be in both places.`,
        text: `Twist: one witness needs an ambulance at the same time as the collector moves the rest of the load. Pick a priority and name an ally for the other. Getting the witness to Majors gives an independent hospital account.`,
        checks: [['sav', 'medium', `First aid until help arrives: recovery position, airway, keep him talking`, `He's breathing steadily when you hand him over.`, `He's in a bad way when the paramedics take over.`],
          ['whl', 'hard', `Drive him to Homerton A&E yourselves`, `Into the ambulance bay in eight minutes. The triage nurse takes him straight through.`, `Stuck at the Morning Lane lights. You make it, but it's close.`],
          ['grt', 'medium', `Carry him down three flights of stairs to the car`, `You make it, legs burning.`, `You have to stop twice.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: Daz finds out`,
    close: `It's Daz who rings, not Moth. His voice is completely different, quiet and cracked. "Jordan," he says. "My little brother. Why didn't anyone tell me?" There's a long silence on the line. Then: "Who switched it?" Another silence. "I want a name. You get me a name, and I'll owe you for the rest of my life." He hangs up before you can answer.`,
    notes: [`The decision: carry on trading, steal the stock, get it pulled off the street, or sell the proof that it was switched.`,
      `The accounts link Owen and Rusk to the switch (E2). The changed booking is a physical prop.`,
      `Daz finding out about Jordan changes him. He becomes either an ally against Rusk or a loose cannon. Play it seriously.`,
      `Telling one faction doesn't mean every rival finds out. Track who knows.`,
      `Handle the harm with care at the table: this is a mission about people getting hurt, not a stunt.`]
  },
  lb5: {
    brief: `A young Lea Boys collector has kept a little black book of made-up debts: who was squeezed, for what, and on whose say-so. Stretch wants the book and the boy. Moth wants the book to use against Stretch. The kid wants to get on a coach at Victoria and never come back. The crew meet the kid at The Coach and Horses, find the duplicate books at the Well Street Loan Office, make or spring the handover at The Red Lion, or take the other road: DS Vale's people at the custody suite. The twist: half the "debts" were invented on Meridian's instructions, squeezing tenants until they'd sign anything.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: the boy with the book`,
    open: `Moth texts a photo: a lad of seventeen in a Lea Boys jacket, holding up a coach ticket like a winning lottery ticket. Then a voice-note. "One of our juniors kept receipts. For debts that never existed." Wind on the line. "Stretch wants the book AND the boy. I want the book. For me." A pause. "Kid's got a coach ticket out tonight. Victoria, 11.40. Get to him first."`,
    cast: ['tessa-price', 'liv-barrett', 'eddie-lane', 'isla-dunn'],
    roles: {'tessa-price': `Moth. Wants the book as leverage against Stretch.`, 'liv-barrett': `Freight clerk at The Coach and Horses who knows the kid and doesn't want him hurt.`, 'eddie-lane': `Cashier at the Well Street Loan Office. Knows which debts are real and which aren't.`, 'isla-dunn': `Response officer at the custody suite. Would take the book and the kid, on terms, and is frightened of being made a scapegoat.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'LY6.3', title: `The Coach and Horses`, read: `The function room at The Coach and Horses on High Road Leyton has a disco ball nobody has switched on since 2004. The kid, Ashley, sits alone at a table with a rucksack between his feet and a pint of Coke, jumping every time the door opens. At the bar, a woman in a freight-yard hi-vis watches him like a big sister. At the other end of the bar, a man in a black puffer watches him like a hawk.`,
        text: `Ashley wants to sell the book and go. Liv Barrett is protecting him. The man in the puffer is Stretch's. The kid will only deal with people who seem to want him safe, not just the book.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'medium', `Convince Ashley you're not Stretch's`, `He shows you the book: names, dates, amounts, and the word "M-instruction" next to half of them.`, `He grabs his rucksack and heads for the fire exit.`],
          ['btl', 'medium', `Stare down Stretch's man at the bar so Ashley can leave`, `The man finishes his drink and goes outside to make a call.`, `He follows Ashley out.`]]},
      {at: 'WS8.4', title: `The Well Street Loan Office`, read: `The Well Street Loan Office is a shopfront with a counter behind bulletproof glass and a ledger store at the back. A cashier in a visor and a cardigan sits behind the glass with a calculator with half the buttons worn blank. "That one's real," Eddie Lane says, very quietly, when you show him a page. "That one isn't. Don't tell Vic I told you."`,
        text: `The duplicate loan ledgers are physical props (two of them) in the ledger store. They prove which debts are real. Eddie will help if nobody knows. Getting into the ledger store means getting past the counter.`,
        checks: [['grf', 'hard', `Get into the ledger store and take both ledgers`, `Two ledgers in a carrier bag, and Eddie looking the other way.`, `One ledger. The second is in the safe.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `In through the rear court window while Eddie keeps the counter busy`, `Through and down without a sound.`, `The window sticks halfway. You're wedged.`]]},
      {at: 'SN4.2', title: `The handover at The Red Lion`, read: `The Red Lion on Stoke Newington Church Street. The snug has frosted glass and a coal fire. Ashley sits by the window, rucksack on his lap. A moped pulls up outside, then another. Ashley sees them, goes white, and bolts for the kitchen passage.`,
        text: `It's an ambush. Stretch's people are here. The book moves with or without a deal. If it turns into a foot chase, it's Moves; if the moped follows, it gets messy.`,
        checks: [['mov', 'hard', `Chase after Ashley through the kitchen passage and down Church Street`, `You catch him at Abney Park gates. He's crying. He gives you the book.`, `He's over the cemetery wall and gone.`],
          ['aim', 'medium', `Hurl a bag of chips at the moped rider's visor as he comes after you`, `Splat. He wobbles into a hedge.`, `It hits the wall. He's still coming.`],
          ['mus', 'medium', `Pull the second rider off his moped`, `He hits the pavement. He's not getting up in a hurry.`, `He kicks you off and keeps going.`]]},
      {at: 'CU4', title: `The other road: the custody suite`, read: `Stoke Newington police station. A front desk, a custody desk, cells down a corridor, and an interview room with a table bolted to the floor. A young officer with a notebook full of sticky tabs comes out to the front desk. "PC Dunn," she says. "You asked for someone who'd listen."`,
        text: `Isla Dunn will take the book and the kid, on terms: a supervised settlement, protection for Ashley and no charges for the crew. She's frightened of being made a scapegoat by her superiors. Walking into a police station voluntarily takes nerve.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'hard', `Walk into a police station voluntarily, with the book`, `You put it on the desk. Isla looks at it, and then at you. "Thank you."`, `You get to the door and turn round.`],
          ['whl', 'medium', `Or get Ashley to Victoria Coach Station by 11.40`, `He's on the coach with two minutes to spare. He waves through the window.`, `11.45. The coach is gone. He sits on the kerb.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: M-instruction`,
    close: `Whoever ends up with it, the book is open on a table somewhere, and the same two words are written next to half the entries in a teenager's careful handwriting: M-INSTRUCTION. Moth reads it over your shoulder. "M," she says. "Meridian." She turns a page, then another. "They told us who to squeeze. Tenants. Six buildings. Squeeze them till they'd sign anything." She shuts the book. "And we did it."`,
    notes: [`The decision: deliver the book, deliver the book and the kid, help him get away, sell it twice, or take it to Vale (through Isla Dunn) for a supervised settlement.`,
      `The duplicate loan ledgers (x2) are physical props.`,
      `The book shows Meridian instructed the Lea Boys to squeeze tenants (E5). It links to BB5, LD4 and MR.`,
      `Ashley is seventeen. Whatever the crew decide, play his fear straight.`,
      `If the handover goes wrong, the driver, the duplicates and whoever ended up holding it keep the trail warm.`]
  },
  lb6: {
    brief: `Stretch has brought home a deal: a partnership with Meridian Renewal. Money for the lads, "consultancy" fees, a future. Moth smells a stitch-up. The crew hear the pitch at the North Park Hospitality Lodge, find the consultancy invoice in a hired suite at the De Beauvoir Block, and get in the room with Evan Lark and the money man at the Quayline Records Suite. The twist: the "consultancy" the Lea Boys would be paid for is clearing tenants out of the six buildings.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: everyone's gassed`,
    open: `Moth sounds tired. "Stretch has shown the lads a deal. Meridian Renewal. Money, 'consultancy', a future." She says "consultancy" like it's a swear word. "Everyone's gassed. Talking about cars. Talking about pensions. Lea Boys with pensions." A long pause. "I'm not gassed. Find out what it actually costs before anyone signs. Please."`,
    cast: ['tessa-price', 'celia-voss', 'yasmin-dale', 'evan-lark'],
    people: {stretch: {}},
    roles: {'tessa-price': `Moth. Wants the truth before the firm signs its soul away.`, 'celia-voss': `Meridian's director. Gives the pitch with her best smile.`, 'yasmin-dale': `Fleet coordinator whose vans delivered the furniture to the De Beauvoir suite. Knows which room.`, 'evan-lark': `In the boardroom with the money man. Has a pen ready.`, stretch: `Excited, for once. Thinks he's finally made it respectable.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'ON8.3', title: `The pitch at the North Park Hospitality Lodge`, read: `The North Park Hospitality Lodge is glass and pale wood, with a view of the Olympic Park. In the private lounge: canapés, a PowerPoint, a putting green on the carpet, and twenty Lea Boys in their best tracksuits holding champagne flutes awkwardly. Celia Voss, in tailored navy with a single pearl, clicks to a slide that says PARTNERSHIP. Stretch is beaming.`,
        text: `Celia pitches. The crew can watch, or get a private word with Celia (she offers one to whoever wins her putting challenge), or slip out to the document office.`,
        checks: [['aim', 'medium', `Win Celia's putting challenge for a private word`, `Hole in one. Celia laughs, genuinely, and takes you aside: "Consultancy means site management, darling. Clearing sites."`, `Off the green and under the canapé table.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `Slip out of the pitch to the document office`, `In and out. The contract has a page missing: "Schedule C — Site Services".`, `A waiter sees you and offers you a canapé, loudly.`]]},
      {at: 'DB3.3', title: `The De Beauvoir Block suite`, read: `The De Beauvoir Block is a shared workspace with studios full of people on laptops and a meeting room on the second floor that has been hired by a company called "East Bank Consultancy Ltd". Yasmin Dale leans on her van in the delivery court. "Delivered the furniture last week," she says. "Second floor, end room. Filing cabinet's locked. Not that I looked."`,
        text: `The consultancy invoice is a physical prop in the filing cabinet. It shows what the Lea Boys would be paid for: "Vacant possession services, six sites, as scheduled."`,
        checks: [['grf', 'medium', `Pick the filing cabinet lock`, `The invoice is on top. "Vacant possession services. Six sites."`, `The lock jams. You'll need to take the whole drawer.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `Get in through the delivery court and up the back stairs`, `Up without being signed in.`, `Reception stops you. "Who are you here to see?"`],
          ['mus', 'easy', `Carry the whole locked drawer out to Yasmin's van`, `Heavy, but you're out the delivery court.`, `It slips on the stairs. Very loud.`]]},
      {at: 'CG8.2', title: `The Quayline Records Suite`, read: `The Quayline Records Suite on Southgate Road is a reception with a fish tank and a boardroom with a long table. Evan Lark sits at one end, smiling, and a man in a quiet grey suit sits at the other with a laptop. "Ah," Lark says. "Moth's friends. Do sit down. We were just about to make Stretch a very rich man."`,
        text: `The money man is Quayline's finance director. They offer the crew a fee of their own to back the deal. Turning down a fortune takes Bottle; a counter-offer takes Front. The crew can renegotiate, back Moth's split or take a fee.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'hard', `Turn down the money man's offer to your face`, `He closes the laptop. "Shame." Lark stops smiling.`, `You hesitate. They both see it.`],
          ['frt', 'medium', `Counter-offer: renegotiate the terms for the Lea Boys`, `"No site services. Security only." Lark agrees, through gritted teeth.`, `Lark laughs. "You're not in a position to negotiate."`],
          ['whl', 'medium', `Get the invoice to Moth before Stretch signs at the Lodge`, `You skid into the car park as Stretch picks up the pen.`, `He's signed by the time you arrive.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: what it costs`,
    close: `Moth reads the invoice on the bonnet of Yasmin's van in the dark. "Vacant possession services," she reads. "Six sites. As scheduled." She looks up. "That's not security. That's clearing people out. Old people. Kids." She folds the invoice. "Stretch wants us to be bailiffs for a property company." She laughs, but there's nothing funny in it. "Lea Boys with pensions."`,
    notes: [`The decision: back the deal, renegotiate, help Moth split the firm, sell the documents, or take the crew's own consultancy fee.`,
      `The consultancy invoice is a physical prop. The property options link together (E1).`,
      `This sets up LB7: Moth challenges Stretch. Note what the crew did.`,
      `A leak changes everything once it is traced or goes public, but the debts are real either way.`]
  },
  lb7: {
    brief: `Coup night. Moth is calling Stretch out in front of the whole firm at the MOTH Club. The crew's reputation could tip it: new boss, same boss, or two firms where there used to be one. They sort out a disputed pledge at a York Hall fight night, gather the members' demands at the Lea Bridge Social Rooms, protect a threatened runner at the Hackney Marshes North Pavilion, and see the vote through. The twist: Stretch has already signed the Meridian deal. Tonight's vote is about whether anyone honours it.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: I'm challenging him`,
    open: `Moth stands in the boxing room at the Social Rooms, taping her own hands for no reason. "I'm challenging him. Out loud. At the MOTH Club. Thursday." She pulls the tape tight. "Stretch don't get to sign us all away." She looks at you. "Your crew's got a name now. People listen to you." She bites the tape off. "Where you stand changes who leads us. Or splits us."`,
    cast: ['tessa-price', 'pearl-okonkwo', 'ollie-grant', 'basil-armitage'],
    people: {stretch: {}, 'daz-riley': {}},
    roles: {'tessa-price': `Moth. Making her challenge. Needs votes.`, 'pearl-okonkwo': `Traders' voice. Her backing tells the members the money will keep flowing.`, 'ollie-grant': `Groundsman at the Hackney Marshes pavilion who is hiding the threatened runner.`, 'basil-armitage': `Chairs the vote again, because he's still neutral and still bored.`, stretch: `Has already signed with Meridian. Needs the vote to make it stick.`, 'daz-riley': `Turns a real debt into a public humiliation to start a fight.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'BG3.3', title: `Fight night at York Hall`, read: `York Hall, Bethnal Green. The home of East End boxing. Tonight it's a white-collar fight night: the ring lit up, the balconies packed, the smell of sweat and Deep Heat. A Lea Boy pledged his car on the result of the third bout and lost. Now he says the fight was fixed and won't hand over the keys. The disputed pledge (the logbook and the keys) is in his kit bag in the changing corridor.`,
        text: `The disputed pledge is a physical prop. It divides the firm: Stretch's lot say pay up, Moth's say the fight was bent. Sorting it fairly earns votes. The promoter offers an exhibition bout: if a crew member goes two rounds, the pledge is settled by the result.`,
        checks: [['mus', 'hard', `Go two rounds in the exhibition bout`, `You're still standing at the bell. The crowd roars. The pledge is settled your way.`, `You're on the canvas in the first. Stretch's lot cheer.`],
          ['grt', 'medium', `Take the punches and stay up, even if you're losing`, `Bloody but standing. Respect from both sides.`, `You go down and stay down.`],
          ['sav', 'medium', `Prove whether the third bout was fixed`, `The ref's corner got paid. You find the bookie's slip in the bin.`, `It looks clean to you.`]]},
      {at: 'CL7.2', title: `The members' demands`, read: `The members' room at the Lea Bridge Social Rooms is packed. Thirty Lea Boys, from teenagers to men in their fifties, all talking at once. Moth stands on a chair with a notebook. "One at a time," she says. "Everyone gets a say. Write it down." She hands you the pen.`,
        text: `Write down the members' demands; they'll matter at the vote. Some want money, some want out of the Meridian deal, some want respect.`,
        checks: [['grt', 'medium', `Listen to every single member, until 2am`, `Thirty demands, written down. Everyone feels heard.`, `You stop at midnight. Ten members feel ignored and vote for Stretch.`],
          ['frt', 'medium', `Turn the demands into a deal both halves could live with`, `A one-page list that half of Stretch's lot would sign.`, `It reads like Moth's manifesto. Stretch's lot won't touch it.`]]},
      {at: 'HM1.4', title: `The runner at the North Pavilion`, read: `The Hackney Marshes North Pavilion at night: floodlights off, football pitches stretching away into the dark. In the club room, a groundsman in a muddy fleece is standing between a terrified sixteen-year-old and the door. "He picked Moth's side," Ollie Grant says. "Somebody wants him to pick again." Outside, three figures are walking across the pitch towards the pavilion.`,
        text: `Protect the runner. Ollie knows the floodlight controls are in the equipment store. The runner's vote matters, and so does his safety.`,
        checks: [['aim', 'medium', `Hit the floodlight switch on the far wall with a thrown football`, `The floodlights blaze on. The three figures freeze, blinded, and turn back.`, `It hits the wall next to it. They keep coming.`],
          ['mus', 'medium', `Stand in the doorway and refuse to move`, `They look at you, and at Ollie's strimmer, and leave.`, `They push through. It's a scrap.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `Get the runner out of the back and across the marshes in the dark`, `Over the fence and away down the Lea.`, `He trips on a ditch. They catch up.`]]},
      {at: 'HC5.4', title: `The vote at the MOTH Club`, read: `The back room at the MOTH Club, gold tinsel still up. Basil Armitage in his cardigan at the head of the table. Stretch on one side, scarf on, calm. Moth on the other, notebook open. Thirty members crammed into the room. Basil taps the table. "Car one. Car two. Let's begin." Then Daz stands up with a sheet of paper. "Before we start," he says loudly, "Kyle here owes me three hundred quid. Tell 'em, Kyle."`,
        text: `Daz turns a real debt into a public humiliation. A scrap breaks out while the members weigh up who'll keep them paid. Then the twist: Stretch admits he's already signed. The vote is whether anyone honours it.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'hard', `Make the challenge in front of everyone`, `The room goes silent. Then, one by one, the members start to nod.`, `Your voice cracks. Stretch smiles.`],
          ['mus', 'medium', `Break up the scrap Daz starts`, `You separate them and sit them down.`, `The scrap spreads. Basil hides under the table.`],
          ['frt', 'medium', `Build a coalition with the members' demands`, `The demands carry. Moth wins, narrowly.`, `It splits down the middle. Two firms.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: who leads`,
    close: `When it's done, Basil reads out the result, slowly, like a dispatcher reading out a job. The room is quiet. Stretch stands, buttons his jacket, and looks at Moth for a long moment. "You'll find out," he says, "that it's harder from the chair." Then he looks at you. "And you. You'll find out too." He walks out. About a third of the room follows him.`,
    notes: [`The decision: keep Stretch, back Moth, take the top job, split the firm, or negotiate a way out. Name the members and the money behind each side.`,
      `The disputed club pledge is a physical prop.`,
      `The new leadership offers the crew LB8. The losers don't vanish: they keep members, debts and witnesses (E5).`,
      `If the crew took the exhibition bout, celebrate it. Boxing at York Hall is a big moment.`]
  },
  lb8: {
    brief: `The final night, from the Lea Boys' side. Meridian is moving on every building at once. The firm can ride the wave, fight it, or sell a chunk of the patch and walk. The crew hear the residents at the Clapton Union Rooms, bring in the traders at Roman Exchange and the households at the Carpenters Breakfast Club, and face the investors at the Meridian Sales Pavilion. Whatever they broker, the members have to accept it. The twist: Daz has been selling the firm's plans to Rusk all along. He's in the car park with Rusk's people.`,
    openTitle: `Read aloud: the price of a patch`,
    open: `The message comes from the firm's group chat, not one person: "THE QUIET HOUR'S HERE." Then Moth, or Stretch, whoever leads now, rings. "We can cash in, stand against it, or sell part of the patch." The voice is steady, but only just. "What we become depends on what our own people will swallow." A pause. "You're at the table now. Don't choke."`,
    cast: ['adele-mensah', 'pearl-okonkwo', 'gwen-price', 'celia-voss'],
    people: {'tessa-price': {}, 'daz-riley': {}, 'martin-rusk': {}},
    roles: {'adele-mensah': `Tenant rep at the Clapton Union Rooms. Wants the Lea Boys gone as much as Meridian.`, 'pearl-okonkwo': `Speaks for the traders at Roman Exchange. Has a price.`, 'gwen-price': `Tenant organiser at the Carpenters Breakfast Club. Speaks for the households.`, 'celia-voss': `Meridian's director. Offers to buy the core patch on its own.`, 'tessa-price': `Moth, or whoever leads the firm now.`, 'daz-riley': `Has been selling the firm's plans to Rusk.`, 'martin-rusk': `In the car park, waiting.`},
    scenes: [
      {at: 'CL8.3', title: `The residents at the Clapton Union Rooms`, read: `The repair office at the Clapton Union Rooms is full: tenants standing, sitting on desks, leaning in the doorway. Adele Mensah stands at the front in her nurse's fleece. "Don't tell me it'll be fine," she says, before you've opened your mouth. "Your lot squeezed us for Meridian. Now you want our help against them?"`,
        text: `The residents want the Lea Boys gone as much as Meridian. Winning them over means honesty about what the firm did.`,
        checks: [['btl', 'medium', `Admit what the firm did, to the whole room`, `Silence. Then Adele nods. "Right. Now we can talk."`, `You try to explain it away. They stop listening.`],
          ['grt', 'easy', `Stay and take every angry question`, `Two hours of it. By the end, they're asking practical questions.`, `You leave early. It looks like running away.`]]},
      {at: 'OF7.2', title: `The traders and the households`, read: `Roman Exchange's trading room, then the Carpenters Breakfast Club's residents' room, in one long night. Pearl Okonkwo with her arms folded, saying "Everybody eats". Gwen Price with her laptop held together with stickers, saying "Two hundred families". Each has a price, and each wants it in writing.`,
        text: `The traders' written demands are a physical prop: collect them at Roman Exchange. Getting between the two sites on the final night takes driving. Each final-night turn, the crew can do one thing: collections, publicity or signatures.`,
        checks: [['frt', 'medium', `Get Pearl's traders to sign up`, `Pearl hands over the written demands. "Everybody eats. Don't forget it."`, `She wants more than you can promise.`],
          ['whl', 'medium', `Drive between Roman Road and Carpenters Road through the final-night chaos`, `Both sites in one turn.`, `Roadworks. You lose a turn.`],
          ['mus', 'easy', `Help Gwen's volunteers set up the breakfast for the households`, `Tea urn on, tables out. Gwen trusts you now.`, `You drop a tray of mugs. She sighs.`]]},
      {at: 'ST7.4', title: `The investors at the Meridian Sales Pavilion`, read: `The signing room at the Meridian Sales Pavilion. Celia Voss with her pen, smiling. An offer on the table to buy the Lea Boys' core patch on its own: the Social Rooms, three lock-ups, a car wash. Through the window, in the car park, you can see Daz leaning against a black car with two men in black fleeces. One of them is Martin Rusk.`,
        text: `The twist: Daz has been selling the firm's plans to Rusk. The offer saves the core business by throwing another household under the bus. Work out what it really costs.`,
        checks: [['sav', 'medium', `Work out what Celia's offer really costs`, `It buys the patch and evicts the Carpenters households. You see it in clause nine.`, `It looks generous. You don't see clause nine.`],
          ['mov', 'medium', `Get out to the car park before Daz drives off with Rusk`, `You're at the car door before it starts.`, `It pulls away as you reach it.`],
          ['aim', 'hard', `Throw your phone, recording, through the car's open window as Daz talks`, `It lands in the footwell. You've got Daz on tape.`, `It bounces off the roof and onto the tarmac.`]]}
    ],
    closeTitle: `Read aloud: what we become`,
    close: `The night ends at the Social Rooms, in the members' room, with whoever is left. The firm is smaller now, or bigger, or different, depending on what you chose. Moth (or Stretch) pours drinks for everyone. "To the Lea Boys," they say, "whatever that means now." Everyone drinks. Then they look at you. "Write it down," they say. "Who owns what. Who owes what. Before anybody forgets."`,
    notes: [`The decision: a smaller street crew, the whole patch, business with Meridian, or sell the patch and get out.`,
      `The traders' written demands are a physical prop.`,
      `Write down the real premises, income, creditors and enemies. Remember the real venues belong to their owners, not the firm (E6).`,
      `Daz selling to Rusk is the twist. What happens to Daz depends on LB4: if his brother was hurt, he may have done it for revenge on the wrong people.`,
      `After this, the endgame runs to MR8, BM8 and NM8.`]
  }
};
